<%- include('partials/header') %>

<section class="section prose">
  <h1>Recipes</h1>
  <p>
    A small, hand-picked set of launch scripts for running a local model on a
    B70 or B65 — three profiles per runtime and backend, and nothing else.
    These are not generated from the results table; they are chosen, and the
    reasoning is written into each file.
  </p>
  <p>
    They are also the exact scripts the benchmark runner launches. Every number
    on this site was produced by one of these files, with only the swept axes
    (context, concurrency, prefill) and greedy decoding overridden — so the
    script you copy is the script that was measured, not an idealised version
    of it.
  </p>

  <h2>The three profiles</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>performance</dt>
    <dd>
      Fastest tokens for a single session. Small context ceiling, big batches.
      Pick this for interactive chat and coding when you are the only user.
    </dd>
    <dt>balanced</dt>
    <dd>
      The one to run if you are not sure. Real context, a few parallel slots,
      headroom left over.
    </dd>
    <dt>context</dt>
    <dd>
      As much context as a 32 GB card will hold, bought with heavier KV
      quantisation and a slower decode. For long documents and RAG.
    </dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>Every recipe, measured</h2>
  <p class="section-note">
    Solo and <%= parallelConcurrency %>-parallel are read off the same short-prefill
    workload so the two numbers are comparable to each other; context is the
    largest size this recipe has actually completed a clean run at, not the
    recipe's configured ceiling.
  </p>

  <% if (recipes.length === 0) { %>
    <p class="empty-state">No recipe files found.</p>
  <% } else { %>
    <div class="recipe-card-grid">
      <% recipes.forEach(function(r) { %>
        <a class="recipe-card link-card" href="/recipes/<%= r.name %>">
          <div class="recipe-card-head">
            <code class="recipe-card-name"><%= r.name %></code>
            <span class="recipe-card-badges">
              <span class="badge badge-tag"><%= r.backend %></span>
              <span class="badge badge-tag"><%= r.runtime %></span>
              <span class="badge badge-tag"><%= r.profile %></span>
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="feature-card-stat-row recipe-card-stats">
            <div class="feature-card-stat">
              <span class="stat-value"><%= fmt.num(r.stats.speedSolo && r.stats.speedSolo.generation_tok_s) %></span>
              <span class="stat-label">tok/s solo</span>
            </div>
            <div class="feature-card-stat">
              <span class="stat-value"><%= fmt.num(r.stats.speedParallel && r.stats.speedParallel.generation_tok_s) %></span>
              <span class="stat-label">tok/s @ <%= parallelConcurrency %>x</span>
            </div>
            <div class="feature-card-stat">
              <span class="stat-value"><%= fmt.int(r.stats.maxContext) %></span>
              <span class="stat-label">max context</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="recipe-card-kv">KV cache: <code><%= r.kvCacheType %></code></p>
          <span class="link-card-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      <% }) %>
    </div>
  <% } %>

  <p class="section-note">
    vLLM has no Vulkan backend on Intel GPUs, so there is no
    <code>vllm-vulkan-*</code> recipe — that combination does not exist, rather
    than being untested. See the
    <a href="/methodology">methodology</a> for what the benchmarks behind these
    do and don't establish, and
    <a href="/submit">submit a result</a> if a recipe performs differently on
    your machine.
  </p>
</section>

<%- include('partials/footer') %>
